    <!-- Push copy is deliberately generic, and this is a privacy rule rather than a
         style choice. A lock screen is readable by whoever is holding the phone, and in
         a shared-phone home that is often not the woman herself. So a care or cycle push
         says only that something is waiting: never whose it is, never that it concerns a
         period or a log, never that a Be Her Sakhi relationship exists at all.

         These used to name the partner and state the health fact outright ("%s logged
         their period today", "%s invited you to Be Her Sakhi", "Your period is expected
         in 2 days"). iOS never did this, and neither did the server: `notify-care-partner`
         sends a silent, data-only push precisely so that nothing is drawn without the app
         being opened. Android was rebuilding a visible notification from that same silent
         payload and putting the detail back on the lock screen.

         Emergency Assistance below is the one deliberate exception, and its wording
         identifies nobody and locates nobody. -->
    <string name="platform_push_care_update">Something new is waiting for you in Sakhi</string>
    <string name="platform_push_sos">Emergency alert -- tap to open</string>
    <!-- Nearby-request push. Deliberately identifies nobody and locates nobody, so it is
         safe on a lock screen. The detail lives behind the responder inbox, which is
         authorised. -->
    <string name="platform_push_emergency_nearby_title">Someone nearby needs help</string>
    <string name="platform_push_emergency_nearby_body">A Sakhi close to you has asked for something. Can you help?</string>
